;; The program's CAFs given up when no live code can reach them (smalls-1123, unpack_sums_6; doc/haskell/HASKELL-EVAL-SURVEY.md,
;; "As built: the program's CAFs collected"). GHC's collector keeps a CAF only while a live closure's or frame's SRT names it, so
;; a top-level list the program floated out (unpack_sums_6's `makeUnlayered magnitude`, a CAF at -O0) is freed cell by cell as
;; its consumer walks it. hsi's globals are Go roots, so an evaluated CAF kept its whole value for the run (10 million
;; cells, 15 GB). Now, when the Go heap has grown past a threshold, the machine marks what its threads, stable pointers, weak
;; pointers and the C API reach (rts.go's marker, following each closure's code to the globals it names, but not the code of
;; a thunk under evaluation: GHC's blackholed thunk has no SRT) and reverts every evaluated CAF of the program's own modules
;; (unit main) that the mark did not reach: unevaluated again, its value left to Go's collector. A reverted CAF entered again
;; is evaluated again, as GHCi's revertCAFs does; by the mark no live code can enter it. The libraries' CAFs (stdout and the
;; other handles, the RTS's closures Go names by name) are never reverted. HSI_CAF_GC=0 turns it off; HSI_CAF_FLOOR_MB sets
;; the floor.


^{:go/pos {:doc [22 1] :tok-pos [23 1]}} (go/type ^{:go/pos {:name-pos [23 6]}} cafRec "cafRec is a CAF of the program: its global, its thunk and its code.\n" ^{:go/pos {:lbrace [23 20] :rbrace [27 1] :struct [23 13]}} (struct
